course: dictionary
lesson: 9581
title: Strawman
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: A strawman is an unfair trick in an argument. Instead of answering what someone really said, you change their idea into a weaker or sillier version, then knock that version down.
explain: «I think we should have less homework.» «So you think children shouldn't learn anything!» The second reply is a strawman, because the speaker never said that. To argue fairly, repeat the other person's real point first, then reply to it.
explain: Strawman has other meanings too. It can be a figure made of straw, like a scarecrow. Informally, a straw man can also be a person used to hide who is really behind something.
fact: A strawman is an unfair trick in an argument: you attack a weaker version of what someone said.
fact: «Strawman» can also mean a figure made of straw, like a scarecrow or a dummy for practice.
fact: (informal) A straw man can be a person used to hide who is really behind something.
fact: Steelmanning is the opposite of strawmanning: it means saying someone's idea in its strongest, fairest way.
fact: Strawman is a noun. In informal use it is also a verb: to strawman someone's idea means to make it sound silly so it is easy to knock down.
exercise: «I think we should have less homework.» «So you think children shouldn't learn anything!» Is the second reply a strawman? | No, it repeats the first idea exactly | *Yes, it changes the idea into a sillier version | No, because it is funny | Yes, because it is a question
exercise: In an argument, what is a strawman? | A figure made of straw in a field | A fair question to check their idea | *An unfair version of the other person's idea, made weak so it is easy to attack | A clear copy of what someone really said
exercise: Which word means stating an idea in its strongest, fairest way? | Strawman | Scarecrow | *Steelmanning | Dummy
test: Sam says, «Dogs are fun to walk.» Jo replies, «So you don't care about cats at all!» Is Jo using a strawman? | No, because cats are fun too | No, Jo is agreeing with Sam | Yes, because Jo is angry | *Yes, Jo changed Sam's point into a sillier one
test: In the sentence «The farmer put a strawman in the field to scare birds», what is a strawman? | A loud noise | *A figure made of straw, like a scarecrow | A fair argument | A person who hides a secret
exam: In an argument, what is the fair way to answer someone? | Ignore their point and change the subject | Make them sound foolish | *Repeat their real point first, then reply to it | Attack the silliest version of their idea
